views {

  // ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  // Two walkthroughs and nothing else.
  //   1 · the life of a tower — a person plans it, the tickets land in the DB, the
  //       loop runs everything to the closed epic.
  //   2 · every failure — the same run with every alarm firing, one step each.
  // ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  // ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // 1 · THE LIFE OF A TOWER
  // ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  dynamic view index {
    title "Binary Obelisk/Walkthrough/1 · The life of a tower — a recipe box, from a vague idea to a closed epic"

    include human, planAgents.*, boCli, knobs, dispatcher, agentMap, spawnEnv, beads,
      tower.*, gitCli, forgeCli, forge, claudeCli, logRun.*, workers.*, stopHook, failHook, waiting.*

    // ═ ACT I — a person plans it, and the tower appears in the database

    human -> planAgents.planEpic "a vague idea: I want somewhere to keep my recipes" {
      title "the judgments the machine never makes"
      notes '''
Nothing dispatches from here yet. `plan-epic` asks what it actually is, researches, and commits to
**one** `epic/<slug>` — the name of the *thing*, two or three lowercase words, no ids and no dates,
because a person reads a branch name and knows which feature it is.

It creates **exactly four** tickets and writes **one** metadata key:
'''
    }

    planAgents.planEpic -> beads "the epic gate, and the three tickets that make it shippable" {
      title "four tickets, one branch key, in this order"
      notes '''
| Ticket | Why it exists | Who does it | Waits on |
|---|---|---|---|
| RECIPE-1 | **the epic itself** — the highest-level statement of what is being built, and the only place `epic/recipe-box` is ever written. A gate: no label, so it dispatches nobody, and the ticket list hides it. Only a human closes it. | — | its three tickets below |
| RECIPE-2 | **assemble the two finished feature branches onto `epic/recipe-box`**, so the journeys and the review are both proved against the assembled result and not against two scattered branches. Writes no code and no tests, opens no pull request, never merges into `main`. | merge | both feature gates |
| RECIPE-3 | **journeys across the whole app** in a real browser on the real stack — state handed between the two features, one success and one failure per feature seen from outside. The thinnest tier; repeats nothing below it. | tester-e2e | RECIPE-2 |
| RECIPE-4 | **the three questions over the whole epic** — does it do what its source of truth demands, do the tests prove it, is it simple and readable. Findings go onto the pull request as comments; it files no tickets and commits nothing. | review | RECIPE-3 |

One key is written here and it is the epic's own branch, copied down verbatim by every row beneath —
never recomputed. No pull-request url: that belongs to the loop. No PR base either: a ticket aims at the
epic's branch *because* it hangs under that epic, and the loop reads the branch off the gate.

Two mistakes here are silent. A gate is a **custom type**: the ticket system accepts an unregistered type
without complaint and simply **hides** it, so a broken gate and a working one look identical until the
tower stalls — the registered types are worth checking before planning. And a ticket carrying zero labels
or two dispatches nothing: the loop never guesses a specialist.
'''
    }

    human -> planAgents.planFeature "break the epic into features" {
      title "one epic in, two features out"
      notes '''
A feature is a complete user-facing flow, never a layer of the app — "the API layer" is not a feature,
"writes a recipe and sees it in the box" is. The agent reads the epic's gates' metadata first (gates are
hidden from the default queries, so a plain child listing answers *"nothing there"* while they all sit
there), maps the repo, then phases the work **foundations → core → dependent → polish**. Here both
features land in core, so nothing orders them and both run side by side.

| Gate | The thing the user gets | Its own branch |
|---|---|---|
| RECIPE-5 | writes a recipe and sees it in the box | `feature/add-recipe` |
| RECIPE-11 | finds a recipe they already saved | `feature/search-recipes` |
'''
    }

    planAgents.planFeature -> beads "per feature: the gate, the suite, the review" {
      title "six tickets written here, and the three edges only this agent can write"
      notes '''
This agent **writes to the database** — three rows per feature, and a tower without them builds and never
ships.

| Ticket | Why it exists | Label → who | Waits on |
|---|---|---|---|
| RECIPE-5 / RECIPE-11 | **the gates.** What the user gets, in the user's words, and the only row that carries `branch: feature/<slug>` — the rows beneath copy it verbatim. No label: a gate dispatches nobody and only a human closes it, and closing it *is* merging the feature branch onto the epic branch. | — | their own three tickets below |
| RECIPE-9 / RECIPE-15 | **the suite.** Proves this feature's pieces are actually wired together in a browser, and that every error and success the feature encapsulates surfaces. One representative case per path, asserted at the seam — never a re-run of the unit tier's matrix, never leaves the feature. | `feature-test` → tester-feature | the last task of the chain — `plan-task` writes that edge |
| RECIPE-10 / RECIPE-16 | **the review.** Reads the branch it is handed, maps the system the change touches, traces it, runs the suites against the instrumented code, answers the three questions, posts findings on the pull request. | `feature-review` → review | the suite |

**Three `blocked-by` edges per feature**, and these are this agent's alone — the tickets either side of
them already existed, and it is the only one standing at both ids:

- the review waits on the suite — nobody certifies a flow that was never run
- the gate waits on the review — a human only ever closes a feature that has been run and certified
- **the epic's merge ticket RECIPE-2 waits on this feature's gate** — one edge per feature

That last edge is the one that gets missed, and it is the reason this agent exists as a step of its own:
`plan-epic` wrote RECIPE-2 long before any feature existed and never sees the pair together. Skip it and
the merge is held by nothing, runs against half-built features, and reports green.

The tickets hang under the epic by a parent-child edge — hierarchy and reading only. A parent-child edge
**never blocks**; only `blocked-by` does, and everything above that blocks because someone standing at both
ids wrote the edge.
'''
    }

    human -> planAgents.planTask "break this one feature into tasks" {
      title "one feature in, its chain out — it refuses without a feature"
      notes '''
Run **once per feature**, and it takes one feature ticket in the prompt or it refuses. It owns exactly
three kinds of ticket: the logic, that logic's micro tests, the UI. The gate, the suite and the review
above them were already written by `plan-feature` — re-creating them would double the tower, and a ticket
that needs two labels is two tickets, not one.
'''
    }

    planAgents.planTask -> beads "RECIPE-6→7→8 and RECIPE-12→13→14 — six tickets, two chains" {
      title "one chain per feature: one branch, one pair of hands"
      notes '''
| Ticket | Why it exists | Label → who | Waits on |
|---|---|---|---|
| RECIPE-6 / RECIPE-12 | **the feature's logic** — domain functions, validators, transformers, the service layer, error factories: inputs, outputs, return shapes, every edge case, the file it lives in, the existing code to follow. The head of the chain, waiting on nothing, so it is claimed first. | `backend-logic` → engineer | — |
| RECIPE-7 / RECIPE-13 | **that one unit's micro tests**, written immediately after the unit it tests and wired to that one alone: every branch, boundary, empty or null input and error, one scenario per behaviour with concrete input→output pairs and the exact test file. The input matrix is enumerated HERE so no tier above repeats it. Back-end only — a component has no ticket on this tier, the suite proves the UI. | `unit-tests` → tester-unit | RECIPE-6 / 12 |
| RECIPE-8 / RECIPE-14 | **the feature's UI** — the whole component: props, state, events, styling, tokens, responsive variants, accessibility, its file path. Built last, so nothing renders against an unproved function. Its ticket stands alone: no micro ticket, no separate styling ticket. | `frontend-ui` → frontend | RECIPE-7 / 13 |

**The chain is the whole of this agent's edge work.** Each ticket waits on the single ticket before it and
nothing else, so the frontier can only ever hand out one ticket of a feature at a time — one branch, one
pair of hands, and the chain cannot run past a broken commit. Its **last** ticket then blocks the suite
ticket `plan-feature` already wrote: the suite proves nothing until every commit of the chain exists.

Three things it gets right or the tower lies:

- **the label is written as the ticket is created**, and the label — never an agent flag — carries the tier; each ticket is read back once before the next is made, because a ticket created without its label is dispatched by nothing and reports nothing
- **the feature's branch is copied verbatim** from its gate onto every row; below its own tier no plan agent ever invents a branch name, and nothing ever recomputes one
- **the two features run side by side because nothing joins them** — that absence of an edge is the parallelism, and an edge written for tidiness is a feature sitting idle while a worker waits
'''
    }

    human -> beads "bd list — all sixteen are in the tower" {
      title "the payment into the database, and the frontier it makes"
      notes '''
Sixteen tickets, sorted by the hand that wrote them: **plan-epic 4** (RECIPE-1…4), **plan-feature 6** (the
two gates, and each gate's suite and review), **plan-task 6** (the two chains of logic, its tests, UI).
`bd list` **hides closed tickets and gates by default**, so the work tickets are what you see — and of
those, exactly two are claimable:

| Claimable | Why |
|---|---|
| RECIPE-6 | head of the Add-a-Recipe chain |
| RECIPE-12 | head of the Search-the-Box chain |

Everything else is held by one `blocked-by` edge, or by an open gate two levels below. Nothing here is
read at runtime by the loop: **the database is the loop's only input** — no design doc, no CLAUDE.md.

| Tier | Tickets |
|---|---|
| planned, invisible to `bd ready` | RECIPE-1 and RECIPE-5 / RECIPE-11 (gates) |
| frontier | RECIPE-6, RECIPE-12 |
| behind one edge | RECIPE-7, 13 → 8, 14 → 9, 15 → 10, 16 → RECIPE-2 → 3 → 4 |
'''
      navigateTo everyFailure
    }

    // ═ ACT II — the run starts, or it refuses to

    human -> boCli "bo loop, from inside the tower" {
      title "one subcommand, in the foreground"
      notes '''
`bo` probes the directory first: `bd list --limit 1 --json` must answer an **array** — `bd` walks *up*
from the cwd and ignores `BEADS_DIR`, so a database in the environment does not save a wrong directory.
Then `bo: dispatching $PWD — Ctrl-C to stop.`, then it `bash`es the engine in the **foreground** so every
tick is on the terminal.

Before the first tick the engine runs five checks that end the run, and one that does not:

| Check | Message |
|---|---|
| `bd jq claude git` on PATH | `<tool> not found on PATH` |
| the three numeric knobs | `BO_<VAR>=<x> is not a number` |
| the poll interval | `BO_POLL_S=<x> is unusable — it must divide 60 (1 2 3 4 5 6 10 12 15 20 30 60)` |
| a beads DB from `$PWD` | `no beads database found from $PWD — cd into the tower project and re-run` |
| the log dir | `cannot create <dir>` |

Each of them walked on its own in the second walkthrough.
'''
      navigateTo everyFailure
    }

    knobs -> dispatcher "4 parallel · 3 s · 0 ticks · ~/.binary-obelisk/logs · labels *" {
      title "read once, at startup"
      notes '''
The poll interval must divide 60 **because** of the waiting lines: they are rate-limited at one a minute
(`60 / BO_POLL_S` ticks), and a poll that does not divide the minute makes that cadence a lie.
`BO_MAX_TICKS` is a test seam; a real run is endless and ends on Ctrl-C.
'''
    }

    dispatcher -> logRun "mkdir -p ~/.binary-obelisk/logs/2026-11-06_09-12-01" {
      title "six files seeded before anything happens"
      notes '''
`claims errors waiting results closes failures` — created up front so a **missing** file and an **empty**
file look the same, and neither can hide a silent failure. `closes` and `failures` are written by the hooks,
possibly long after this run ends, into the dir this run made.
'''
    }

    // ═ ACT III — one tick, end to end, with every parameter named

    dispatcher -> beads "step 2 — bd ready --claim --json" {
      title "the only atomic claim in the system"
      notes '''
Before it, step 0 reads the branches already at work (`bd list --status in_progress --limit 0 --json` →
`.metadata.branch | sort -u`) **before** claiming, so the ticket in hand is never counted against itself;
step 1 counts live background sessions with `claude agents --json` and does not claim at all at four.

```json
[{ "id": "RECIPE-6", "title": "validateRecipe + saveRecipe — pure functions",
   "status": "in_progress", "assignee": "brodie", "issue_type": "task",
   "labels": ["backend-logic"], "parent": "RECIPE-5",
   "metadata": { "branch": "feature/add-recipe" }, "lease_expires_at": "…+5 min" }]
```

Two facts come out of this one payload and **nothing reads the ticket's text**: the label that picks the
specialist, and the branch the work lands on. Atomic is not a detail — assignee, status and lease are set
in one step, so a second loop cannot take the same ticket.
'''
    }

    dispatcher -> agentMap "step 3 — backend-logic matches exactly one key" {
      title "the only label→worker binding in the system"
      notes '''
`backend-logic` is the only member of `.[0].labels` that is both a key of `AGENT` and in the allowed set,
so the answer is `engineer`. Zero or two matches is the tower's bug, not the loop's: the loop never guesses
a specialist. The trap is a ticket labelled with its **category** (`frontend-ux` instead of `frontend-ui`) —
that matches nothing, and reads exactly like a ticket with no labels at all.
'''
    }

    dispatcher -> beads "step 6 — walk the parent chain for the PR base" {
      title "RECIPE-6 → RECIPE-5 → RECIPE-1; read the gate's own branch"
      notes '''
`.[0].parent` → `epicGateOf` (≤ 4 hops of `bd show --json` `parent`) → the gate's own `branch` =
`epic/recipe-box`. **The base is not stored on the ticket** — a copy could drift from the gate it copied and
nothing compares the two, so the loop once published one branch while the hook aimed at the other. The tower
already says which epic a ticket belongs to; the chain names the gate and the gate is read there.
`main` when the claim's parent **is** that topmost gate.
'''
    }

    dispatcher -> forgeCli "step 7 — the epic gate has no pr: publish its branch" {
      title "ensureEpicGatePr RECIPE-1"
      notes '''
The gate's `pr` is empty, so this is the loop's job, once, before any worker is spawned:
`push -u origin epic/recipe-box` off `main`, `gh pr create --base main --head epic/recipe-box`, then
`bd update RECIPE-1 --set-metadata pr=<url>`. Only a URL matching `/(pull|merge_requests)/[0-9]+` counts —
an error message quoting a docs URL is not a pull request.

Every feature PR below aims at that branch, so if any of it fails nothing beneath the epic has a base:
`ERROR reason=epic-pr-failed step=…`, left claimed, seven steps name where it died.
'''
    }

    forgeCli -> forge "PR 1 — epic/recipe-box → main" {
      title "the epic's pull request, published by the loop"
      notes '''
Which forge this is, `detectForge` settles three ways in order: an honest host name in the remote URL; then
the refs the host actually publishes (only GitHub serves `refs/pull/`, only GitLab `refs/merge-requests/`);
then a machine holding **exactly one** of `gh`/`glab`. Where all three are ambiguous the hook refuses to
guess — a wrong guess pushes a branch to the wrong forge.
'''
    }

    dispatcher -> gitCli "step 8 — git worktree add -B feature/add-recipe …/feature+add-recipe origin/epic/recipe-box" {
      title "a worktree belongs to a branch, not a ticket"
      notes '''
Under `<tower>/.claude/worktrees/`, the folder is the branch with `/` → `+`. `<start>` is the first of
`origin/<branch>`, `origin/<prTarget>`, `<prTarget>`, `HEAD` that resolves — **a feature branch starts where
it is going to land**. A branch already checked out somewhere is reused, never re-added (`git worktree list
--porcelain` scanned for `refs/heads/<branch>`), which is the same rule the loop applies to workers.
'''
    }

    dispatcher -> spawnEnv "step 9 — eight BO_* on the spawn line" {
      title "this is how the hooks know what to finish"
      notes '''
| Var | Value |
|---|---|
| `BO_TICKET` | `RECIPE-6` — and the **only** test that tells a dispatched worker from any other session in any other project |
| `BO_BRANCH` | `feature/add-recipe` |
| `BO_PR_TARGET` | `epic/recipe-box` |
| `BO_AGENT` / `BO_LABEL` | `engineer` / `backend-logic` |
| `BO_PROJECT_DIR` | the tower root — **the worktree is not the tower** |
| `BO_LOG_DIR` | `~/.binary-obelisk/logs/2026-11-06_09-12-01` |
| `BO_DONE_FILE` | `…/done-RECIPE-6` — `rm -f`d before the spawn, so a marker can only mean *this* session |

Without them the worker simply stops and its ticket sits `in_progress`.
'''
    }

    dispatcher -> claudeCli "claude --bg --agent engineer --permission-mode bypassPermissions" {
      title "`cd` inside the worktree; deliberately no -w"
      notes '''
`claude --worktree` only ever **creates** a worktree, and this branch already owns its own. The session id is
read back out of that worker's own `spawn-RECIPE-6` file (`backgrounded · 287d3c4c`) — one shared `last-spawn`
used to be overwritten by every spawn, so the id survived only for the run's last worker, the one thing a human
needs to attach to. Workers run unattended with `bypassPermissions`: this loop belongs only in a repo you fully trust.
'''
    }

    claudeCli -> workers.engineer "the six rules are the prompt itself" {
      title "buildWorkerPrompt — not a file the worker has to go looking for"
      notes '''
The dispatcher's own prompt carries the contract, and the same contract is the `bead-heartbeat` skill every
worker loads first: heartbeating your ticket between long steps, never closing your own ticket, putting a
comment on the ticket saying what remains when the brief outruns you, spawning nothing, staying on your branch
in the folder you were given, and `touch $BO_DONE_FILE` as your very last action **only when the brief is truly
done**. Rule 5 has one branch for `merge`, which is granted `git merge`.

```bash
bd heartbeat RECIPE-6          # between long steps — the lease is five minutes
```

The lease lapsing does not hand the ticket to anyone: nothing in this plugin runs `bd reclaim`. A lapsed lease
leaves the ticket parked `in_progress`, off the frontier, until a person moves it.
'''
    }

    workers.engineer -> beads "bd heartbeat RECIPE-6 · report in the final message · close nothing" {
      title "the worker's only writes are a heartbeat and a note"
      notes '''
The engineer writes `validate-recipe.ts`, `types.ts`, `ports.ts`, heartbeats around the long steps, and reports.
It leaves the ticket **open** — closing is a human act done after reading the report; a ticket a worker closes
itself closes unverified.
'''
    }

    workers.engineer -> logRun.fDone "touch $BO_DONE_FILE — the last action" {
      title "the one file the Stop hook is fail-closed on"
      notes '''
The session ending is not the statement; the marker is. Everything the next step does depends on this file's
existing.
'''
    }

    // ═ ACT IV — the hook that refused to trust them

    claudeCli -> stopHook "Stop fires in that session (timeout 300)" {
      title "hooks.json registers it for every session of every project"
      notes '''
Registered on `Stop`, so it fires everywhere the plugin is enabled — `BO_TICKET` is what tells a dispatched
worker apart from everything else, and a session the loop did not spawn leaves on the first line:

```bash
[[ -n "${BO_TICKET:-}" ]] || exit 0
```

Then the sequence, each step able to refuse:

```
commit onto its branch → push → the branch's PR → write the url onto the ticket → close it
```

Fifteen refusals, all `exit 0`, all leaving the ticket exactly where it was. All fifteen walked in the second
walkthrough; here is the honest path.
'''
      navigateTo everyFailure
    }

    stopHook -> beads "bd show --json --long RECIPE-6 → the title" {
      title "the ticket's state is verified, not assumed"
      notes '''
Not `in_progress` and the hook is out: state `closed` means this is a second Stop in a session that already
shipped, and it leaves **silently** rather than committing onto a shared branch a second time. Then `BO_BRANCH`,
`BO_PR_TARGET`, and `git branch --show-current` must equal `BO_BRANCH` exactly — the worker was told never to
change branches, and this is the last place that can refuse to file its work under the wrong ticket's name.
'''
    }

    stopHook -> gitCli "git add -A · git commit -F - · git push -u origin feature/add-recipe" {
      title "the commit message IS the ticket title"
      notes '''
```
validateRecipe + saveRecipe — pure functions

Ticket: RECIPE-6
```

One commit per task, onto one branch — the tasks under a feature are **commits**, never branches. A review or
test ticket may legitimately land nothing: `git diff --cached --quiet` skips the commit and the hook carries on
to push, PR and close.
'''
    }

    stopHook -> forgeCli "gh pr list --head feature/add-recipe --base epic/recipe-box --state open" {
      title "one branch, one pull request, forever"
      notes '''
None yet, so `gh pr create --base epic/recipe-box --head feature/add-recipe --title "<ticket title>"`. The next
task on this branch finds **this** PR and reuses it — which is why the branch, not the ticket, is the identity
of the work, and why the whole line had to be chained onto one branch.
'''
    }

    forgeCli -> forge "PR 2 — feature/add-recipe → epic/recipe-box" {
      title "the epic gate's branch, not main"
      notes '''
The base came down as `BO_PR_TARGET`, which the loop read off the epic gate. Nothing on the ticket says it, so
nothing can disagree with the gate.
'''
    }

    stopHook -> beads "bd update --set-metadata pr=… · bd close --session 287d3c4c --reason \"worker session … finished; PR …\"" {
      title "written before closed, and closed as its claimant"
      notes '''
`--set-metadata` **merges**, so the hook's `pr` never disturbs the plan's `branch` key. Then the close: `bd`
refuses to close a ticket whose assignee is not the actor, and the **dispatcher** claimed this one, so the hook
deliberately passes the real `session_id` rather than overriding the actor. If the annotation fails the hook
stops rather than closing a ticket whose PR is unfindable.
'''
    }

    stopHook -> logRun.fCloses "CLOSES … url=https://github.com/…/pull/2" {
      title "proof of work, joinable by grep alone"
      notes '''
Every line of every file carries the same nine keys (`run repo ticket label agent branch target session worktree`),
`"-"` for whatever is unknown — so this line leads back to the `CLAIMS reason=dispatched` line, the worker's `spawn-*`
file and any `errors` alarm by `grep` alone, with no parser in between.
'''
    }

    // ═ ACT V — the chain releases, feature by feature, both branches at once

    dispatcher -> beads "the next tick — RECIPE-7 is ready, then 8, 9, 10" {
      title "closing a ticket's blocker is the only thing that releases a ticket"
      notes '''
Nothing in the loop "knows" the order; the `blocked-by` graph is the only enforcer:

| Closed | Releases | Worker | On branch |
|---|---|---|---|
| RECIPE-6 | RECIPE-7 | tester-unit | `feature/add-recipe` |
| RECIPE-7 | RECIPE-8 | frontend | same |
| RECIPE-8 | RECIPE-9 | tester-feature | same |
| RECIPE-9 | RECIPE-10 | review | same |

Each is the same tick you just walked — claim, match, folder, spawn, hook — and each is a **second commit** on
the same branch, into the same PR, with one pair of hands on it at a time. The tiers never overstep each other:
the micro tier is exhaustive on one back-end function alone and never touches a component; the feature tier
proves the pieces are wired and never re-runs that matrix; the epic tier walks journeys and duplicates nothing
below it.
'''
    }

    dispatcher -> workers.testerUnit "RECIPE-7 — the micro tier, same branch" {
      title "the second commit onto feature/add-recipe"
      notes '''
Every branch, every boundary (120 **and** 121 characters), empty and null, each error `validateRecipe` throws,
via `framework-jest` — the agent owns the tier, the skill owns the tool, so no test agent has a framework opinion
of its own. Its final message reports the count; the Stop hook files it.
'''
    }

    dispatcher -> workers.testerFeature "RECIPE-9 — the composition tier" {
      title "the pieces are proved wired, against the finished UI"
      notes '''
One representative case per path, asserted at the seam, the encapsulated errors surfacing to the user — deliberately
not exhaustive, and never a re-run of RECIPE-7's matrix. Nothing is assembled and nothing is merged: it is proved
on the feature branch, because the branch **is** the feature.
'''
    }

    dispatcher -> workers.reviewAgent "RECIPE-10 — the three questions" {
      title "the only worker whose skills are one contract deep"
      notes '''
Does the code do what its source of truth demands; do the tests prove it; is it simple and readable. It maps the
system the change sits in (entry → exit, following imports), instruments it with trace logs, runs the tests against
the instrumented code, and posts findings as PR comments — filing no tickets, committing nothing, the trace logs
staying in the working tree. Only `bead-heartbeat` is preloaded; `bead-read`, the four standards and `pr-comments`
load on demand at the step that uses them.
'''
    }

    dispatcher -> workers.engineer "and RECIPE-12 was already at work the whole time" {
      title "two features, two branches, four sessions"
      notes '''
`feature/search-recipes` ran side by side with this one from the first tick, on its own folder and its own PR —
`branch_busy` never once fired, because the graph made each feature a single file of work. At four live sessions
the tick stops claiming and writes `max_parallel in_progress=…` instead; that is not an error, and it is a minute
between its lines.
'''
    }

    // ═ ACT VI — the tower stops, and only a person can start it again

    dispatcher -> waiting.towerStalled "WAITING … reason=tower_stalled waiting=2" {
      title "the loop is finished and cannot know it"
      notes '''
Both feature chains are closed; RECIPE-11 and RECIPE-16's gates are open and hold the epic tier off the frontier.
The claim came back empty, so the probe ran `bd list --status open,blocked --limit 0 --json`, found two work tickets
that exist but are blocked, and wrote this instead of the calm line:

| Line | Means |
|---|---|
| `frontier_empty` | nothing to do — the tower is finished or unplanned |
| `tower_stalled waiting=N` | work **does** exist and something is holding it — usually an open gate |

A broken tower must never read as a calm one. The loop does not end, does not retry, and does not nag: one line a
minute per reason, forever, until a human acts.
'''
      navigateTo everyFailure
    }

    human -> tower.featA "read PR 2, verify the flow, close RECIPE-5" {
      title "closing the gate IS merging the branch"
      notes '''
There is **no `human` agent**: the person enters through gates, and nothing is ever spawned for them; a claimable
ticket assigned to a human is a model bug. The gate's PR is the feature branch, so closing RECIPE-5 is the merge of
`feature/add-recipe` into `epic/recipe-box`. Workers report, humans close — this is the only place a claim loop
cannot fake understanding, which is why the tower is *supposed* to stall here.
'''
    }

    human -> tower.featB "read PR 3, close RECIPE-11" {
      title "the second gate, and now the epic tier is unblocked"
      notes '''
Both feature gates closed → RECIPE-2's two `blocked-by` edges are gone, and the merge ticket is the frontier.
'''
    }

    dispatcher -> workers.mergeAgent "RECIPE-2 — assemble, then the e2e tier, then the review" {
      title "the epic is the only place two branches ever meet"
      notes '''
The merge agent reads the feature branches off the **feature gates' own `branch` keys** — it never derives a branch
from a title — replays them onto `epic/recipe-box` one at a time in the order its brief gives, resolves what collides
between them from the `diff3` base (never from one side), runs the project's own test command and **reads the count**
(exit 0 while the runner printed `no tests` is the suite saying *I am configured wrong and cannot tell you*). No new
code, no new tests, no merge into `main`, no PR of its own. Then RECIPE-3 walks the journeys across both features on
the assembled result, and RECIPE-4 asks the three questions of the whole epic.
'''
    }

    human -> tower.epic "close RECIPE-1 — the thumbs-up on PR 1 into main is yours" {
      title "the epic gate: closed once, by you, after epic-review"
      notes '''
The tower is complete: 16 tickets, 3 pull requests, 2 branches merged into the epic branch, 1 PR into main. Nothing
in the system ever deleted a branch, unclaimed a closed ticket, or closed a gate.

| What the loop did | What the human did |
|---|---|
| published `epic/recipe-box` + PR 1, made 4 worktrees, spawned 14 workers, wrote the epic gate's `pr` | planned it, verified 3 PRs, closed 3 gates |
| closed 14 tickets through one fail-closed hook | closed the 3 gates no worker could reach |
'''
    }

    autoLayout LeftRight
  }

  // ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // 2 · EVERY FAILURE
  // ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  dynamic view everyFailure {
    title "Binary Obelisk/Walkthrough/2 · Every failure — the same tower with every alarm firing"

    include human, boCli, knobs, dispatcher, beads, agentMap, gitCli, forgeCli, forge, claudeCli,
      logRun.*, workers.*, stopHook, failHook,
      alarms.startupAlarms.*, alarms.tickAlarms.*, alarms.stopAlarms.*, alarms.failAlarms.*, waiting.*

    // ═ PART A — the run never starts (five of these, then one that lies to you)

    dispatcher -> alarms.startupAlarms.toolsMissing "bd jq claude git — <tool> not found on PATH · exit 1" {
      notes '''
No defaults, no partial run. Note the Stop hook carries its **own, shorter** list — `bd jq git` — because it
never spawns a session; a machine with `claude` missing runs a tower that can never spawn and never says so at startup.
'''
    }

    dispatcher -> alarms.startupAlarms.knobNotNumeric "BO_MAX_PARALLEL=four is not a number · exit 1" {
      notes '''
Digit-checked on all three knobs. A knob typed wrong would otherwise change how many unattended sessions run with
`bypassPermissions`, so it is not defaulted silently.
'''
    }

    dispatcher -> alarms.startupAlarms.pollUnusable "BO_POLL_S=7 is unusable — it must divide 60 · exit 1" {
      notes '''
`1 2 3 4 5 6 10 12 15 20 30 60`. The waiting lines are one a minute per reason, which is `60 / BO_POLL_S` ticks;
a poll of 7 makes that promise false, so the run refuses rather than lie about its own cadence.
'''
    }

    dispatcher -> alarms.startupAlarms.noBeadsDb "no beads database found from $PWD · exit 1" {
      notes '''
`bd list --limit 1 --json` must answer an array — a real probe of "can anything be done here", not a look for a
`.beads` folder. `bd` walks **up** from the cwd and ignores `BEADS_DIR`, so a database in the environment does not
save a wrong directory, and a tower two levels up silently becomes the tower.
'''
    }

    dispatcher -> alarms.startupAlarms.logDirUnwritable "cannot create ~/.binary-obelisk/logs/… · exit 1" {
      notes '''
Fatal on purpose: a run that cannot write its logs cannot raise an alarm, and a silent dispatcher is the one thing
this design refuses to be.
'''
    }

    dispatcher -> alarms.startupAlarms.labelsStarved "BO_ALLOWED_LABELS=frontend-ux — the run starts and does nothing" {
      title "the startup mistake that is NOT fatal"
      notes '''
A typo'd label narrows the allowed set to something no ticket carries, so every claim matches zero keys, every claim
becomes `label-ambiguous`, and the tower sits still while the waiting lines keep arriving on schedule. Diagnosis is
by hand: compare `bd list --json`'s labels against the eight keys of the `AGENT` map.
'''
    }

    // ═ PART B — the tick fails: and the ticket's fate is the interesting part

    dispatcher -> alarms.tickAlarms.bdFailure "ERROR reason=bd-failure exit=2 msg=\"…\"" {
      title "the ticket's fate: nothing — there was no claim"
      notes '''
Two forms, one reason: `bd ready --claim` exited non-zero (last line of `bd-stderr` quoted), or exited `0` and handed
back no `id` (first 60 characters of the payload quoted). The tick returns and the next tick tries again. An **empty**
answer is not this alarm — that is a waiting line, and confusing the two turns every idle minute into a false fire.
'''
    }

    dispatcher -> beads "the claim came back empty — bd list --status open,blocked tells which line to write" {
      title "the fork that decides whether a broken tower looks calm"
      notes '''
No off-frontier work → `frontier_empty`. Work exists but is blocked → `tower_stalled waiting=N`. Two alarms, not one,
because they mean opposite things.
'''
      navigateTo index
    }

    dispatcher -> alarms.tickAlarms.labelAmbiguous "ERROR reason=label-ambiguous matched=\"\" labels=\"frontend-ux\" · LEFT CLAIMED" {
      notes '''
Zero **or two** dispatching labels. The ticket is left claimed so its five-minute lease parks it off the frontier —
otherwise the loop spins on the same unworkable ticket every tick — and the errors file is the alarm. The commonest
cause is a ticket labelled with its **category** instead of its label, which is indistinguishable from having none.
'''
      navigateTo index
    }

    dispatcher -> alarms.tickAlarms.metadataMissing "ERROR reason=metadata-missing · LEFT CLAIMED" {
      notes '''
The claim's `metadata` has no `branch` — a plan agent skipped its one key. There is no folder to work in and nothing
to push, so the loop never invents a branch: a plan bug stays a plan bug.
'''
    }

    dispatcher -> beads "reason=branch-busy → bd unclaim RECIPE-7 and try the next claim" {
      title "one pair of hands per branch · PUT BACK"
      notes '''
The branch was already at work, so the claim goes straight back and its id joins `skippedTicket`; at most
`BO_MAX_PARALLEL + 2` = six attempts a tick, then the waiting line `branch_busy held=N`.

This is the **fallback**, not the mechanism: the graph makes a feature one linked line, so the frontier can only hand
out one task per feature. This alarm is how you discover a tower that was never chained.
'''
    }

    dispatcher -> alarms.tickAlarms.epicPrFailed "ERROR reason=epic-pr-failed step=push gate=RECIPE-1 gateBranch=epic/recipe-box · LEFT CLAIMED" {
      title "seven steps name where it died"
      notes '''
| step | when |
|---|---|
| `walk` | `epicGateOf` reached no top of the parent chain within 4 hops |
| `gate-branch` | the epic gate carries no `branch` of its own |
| `forge` | `detectForge` settled on nothing |
| `missing-tool` | the settled forge's CLI is not installed |
| `push` | the epic branch could not be published off `main` |
| `pr` | no URL matching `/(pull\|merge_requests)/<digits>` came back |
| `annotate` | `bd update --set-metadata pr=` refused |

Left claimed every time, and correctly: a worker spawned under an epic branch that does not exist burns a slot and
files a commit nobody can pull. The epic branch appears only as `gateBranch=` — never `branch=`, which belongs to the
claim, and the two differ.
'''
    }

    dispatcher -> alarms.tickAlarms.worktreeFailed "ERROR reason=worktree-failed branch=feature/add-recipe · LEFT CLAIMED" {
      notes '''
None of `origin/<branch>`, `origin/<prTarget>`, `<prTarget>`, `HEAD` resolved, or `git worktree add` refused (a folder
already there for another branch). Same treatment as a mislabelled ticket: a worker spawned into a missing folder burns
a slot on nothing.
'''
    }

    dispatcher -> alarms.tickAlarms.workerNotDispatched "ERROR reason=worker-not-dispatched msg=\"…\" · bd unclaim" {
      title "the one tick alarm that puts the ticket straight back"
      notes '''
`claude --bg` exited non-zero and the last line of that worker's `spawn-<ticket>` file is quoted. Nothing was ever
spawned to work this ticket, so unlike every other tick alarm it is returned at once — the next tick may well succeed.
This is also the shape of a model that has run out of agents: `claude` refuses an `--agent` name that is not a file in
`agents/`, and the reason is in that file.
'''
    }

    // ═ PART C — the Stop hook refuses fifteen times, in code order

    stopHook -> alarms.stopAlarms.missingToolHook "ERROR reason=missing-tool tool=git" {
      title "1 of 15 · the ticket stays claimed"
      notes '''
Checked before anything the hook does — but after the `BO_TICKET` guard, so a session the loop never spawned still
exits on line one rather than alarming about someone else's project.
'''
    }

    stopHook -> alarms.stopAlarms.noWorktreeHook "ERROR reason=no-worktree" {
      title "2 of 15"
      notes '''
No `.cwd` on stdin, or that directory does not exist. The hook will not guess the worktree from its own directory:
guessing here means committing whatever happens to be in some other project's tree.
'''
    }

    stopHook -> alarms.stopAlarms.ticketNotInWork "ERROR reason=ticket-not-in-work state=open" {
      title "3 of 15 · and silent when state=closed"
      notes '''
`closed` means this is a second Stop in a session that already shipped — the hook leaves **silently** (`exit 0`), because
committing onto a shared branch a second time is exactly the thing the branch needs protection from. Anything else
(`not-found`, `open`) is an alarm.
'''
    }

    stopHook -> alarms.stopAlarms.noBranchHook "ERROR reason=no-branch" {
      title "4 of 15"
      notes '''
`BO_BRANCH` was not on the spawn line, so the loop never got as far as reading the claim's branch.
'''
    }

    stopHook -> alarms.stopAlarms.noPrTarget "ERROR reason=no-pr-target" {
      title "5 of 15"
      notes '''
`BO_PR_TARGET` was not on the spawn. Named for the target because `pr` now means the url: an absent target means the
hook is aiming blind, and it will not open a pull request into a guess.
'''
    }

    stopHook -> alarms.stopAlarms.notDeclaredDone "ERROR reason=not-declared-done" {
      title "6 of 15 · the gate of the whole design"
      notes '''
`$BO_DONE_FILE` does not exist: the session ended without saying, out loud, in the one place both programs read, that
its brief is finished. **No commit, no push, no PR, no close.** The ticket stays claimed, its lease parks it off the
frontier, and every task chained behind it waits.

That is the whole safety of a shared branch: a half-finished task cannot slide its neighbours sideways. And the converse
is the one failure this model cannot undo on its own — a worker that touches the marker early files half a task onto a
branch its siblings are queued behind, and nothing can see it.
'''
    }

    stopHook -> alarms.stopAlarms.wrongBranch "ERROR reason=wrong-branch want=feature/add-recipe got=detached" {
      title "7 of 15"
      notes '''
The worker was told never to change branches; if it did anyway, this is the last place that can refuse to file its work
under the wrong ticket's name. A detached tree reads as `got="detached"`.
'''
    }

    stopHook -> alarms.stopAlarms.noTitle "ERROR reason=ticket-has-no-title" {
      title "8 of 15"
      notes '''
The commit message IS the ticket title, so a ticket with no title cannot be filed — the commit would be anonymous on a
shared branch.
'''
    }

    stopHook -> alarms.stopAlarms.commitFailed "ERROR reason=commit-failed" {
      title "9 of 15 · and an empty tree is NOT this"
      notes '''
`git add -A` then `git commit -F -`. A review or test ticket legitimately lands nothing: `git diff --cached --quiet`
skips the commit and the hook carries on to push, PR and close — so this alarm means a genuine failure, not an empty tree.
'''
    }

    stopHook -> alarms.stopAlarms.pushFailed "ERROR reason=push-failed msg=\"…\"" {
      title "10 of 15"
      notes '''
`git push -u origin <BO_BRANCH>` failed; the first 160 characters of its output are the `msg=`. The commit is filed
locally and the ticket stays claimed — nothing is rolled back, because a local commit is evidence, not a mistake to hide.
'''
    }

    stopHook -> alarms.stopAlarms.forgeUnknown "ERROR reason=forge-unknown remote=<url>" {
      title "11 of 15 · reached only after the commit and the push"
      notes '''
The remote name lied, it publishes neither `refs/pull/` nor `refs/merge-requests/`, and the machine holds both `gh` and
`glab` — or neither. The hook refuses to guess: a wrong guess pushes a branch to the wrong forge.
'''
    }

    stopHook -> alarms.stopAlarms.forgeToolMissing "ERROR reason=missing-tool tool=gh forge=github" {
      title "12 of 15 · the same reason as alarm 1, a different tool="
      notes '''
The forge settled, its CLI is not installed. The `forge=` suffix is how you tell this apart from the up-front check in
the errors file.
'''
    }

    stopHook -> alarms.stopAlarms.prNotCreated "ERROR reason=pr-not-created forge=github" {
      title "13 of 15"
      notes '''
Either the list of this branch's existing PRs and the create both failed, or the CLI printed an error quoting some other
URL — only `/(pull|merge_requests)/<digits>` counts as a pull request.
'''
    }

    stopHook -> alarms.stopAlarms.ticketNotAnnotated "ERROR reason=ticket-not-annotated pr=<url>" {
      title "14 of 15 · the work is out there, the ticket cannot say where"
      notes '''
`bd update --set-metadata pr=<url>` failed, so the hook **stops** rather than closing a ticket whose PR is unfindable.
('--set-metadata merges, so this is the one place the hook could have disturbed the plan's `branch` key and does not.)
'''
    }

    stopHook -> alarms.stopAlarms.closeFailed "ERROR reason=close-failed pr=<url>" {
      title "15 of 15 · everything else already succeeded"
      notes '''
The commit, the push and the PR are all out there with no record in the tower. `bd` refuses to close a ticket whose
assignee is not the actor and the **dispatcher** claimed this one, so the hook passes the real `--session <session_id>`
rather than overriding the actor — when that session id is stale or wrong, this is where it surfaces.
'''
    }

    // ═ PART D — the session dies instead of finishing

    claudeCli -> failHook "StopFailure fires — .error and .last_assistant_message on stdin" {
      title "the reason is handed over, never polled or guessed"
      notes '''
The harness fires this hook itself when a worker's session dies mid-turn on an API error. `.error` is its coarse label
for the kind of death (often just `unknown`); `.last_assistant_message` is the last thing the session produced, and for
local inference that is where the real text is — typically an `API Error:` line from the proxy.

```bash
bd update "$ticket" --append-notes "Worker session <id> failed: <kind>
Last message: <last assistant message>"
bd update "$ticket" --status failed-agent
```

The reason goes on **before** the status changes. A dead worker stops heartbeating and its lease lapses, and nothing in
this plugin runs `bd reclaim` — so an unwatched lapse leaves the ticket parked `in_progress`, invisible to `bd ready`,
until a person moves it. This hook is what takes it somewhere a human will look, on purpose.
'''
    }

    failHook -> alarms.failAlarms.ticketNotFoundFail "ERROR reason=ticket-not-found" {
      title "and silent when the state is not in_progress"
      notes '''
`bd show --json` returned no state at all. Already parked, already shipped, anything not `in_progress` is somebody
else's business — a dead session must not rewrite a ticket another worker owns.
'''
    }

    failHook -> alarms.failAlarms.failNotAnnotated "ERROR reason=ticket-not-annotated kind=unknown" {
      title "so the status is never touched"
      notes '''
The `--append-notes` failed, so the hook stops: a ticket parked in failure with nothing said about why is worse than one
left `in_progress`.
'''
    }

    failHook -> alarms.failAlarms.statusNotSet "ERROR reason=status-not-set kind=api_error from=in_progress" {
      title "the shape that looks like a live worker and is a dead one"
      notes '''
The reason is on the ticket and the status is still `in_progress`. Nothing here brings it back — no `bd reclaim` runs in
this plugin — so it sits out of `bd ready` until a person moves it, and the **missing** `failures` line is the tell that
this happened.
'''
    }

    // ═ PART E — not failures: the ticks that had nothing to dispatch

    dispatcher -> waiting.maxParallel "WAITING … reason=max_parallel in_progress=4 working=4 next=RECIPE-12" {
      title "the workers are working; the tick simply does not claim"
      notes '''
The capacity count comes fresh from `claude agents --json` every tick — never from a counter the loop keeps — precisely
so a session that died without anyone noticing cannot hold a slot forever.
'''
    }

    dispatcher -> waiting.maxParallelStalled "WAITING … reason=max_parallel_stalled in_progress=0 working=4" {
      title "at capacity with nothing in progress: the slots are ghosts"
      notes '''
Four sessions counted busy, no ticket in progress. This is the state the live recount exists to make **visible**; the two
counts disagreeing is the whole alarm.
'''
    }

    dispatcher -> waiting.branchBusyWaiting "WAITING … reason=branch_busy held=3 next=…" {
      title "every claim this tick wanted an occupied branch"
      notes '''
Three claims unclaimed and skipped inside the six-attempt limit. On a chained tower this line should never appear — its
appearance says the tower was built as a fan.
'''
    }

    dispatcher -> waiting.frontierEmpty "WAITING … reason=frontier_empty next=-" {
      title "the calm one — finished, or never planned"
      notes '''
Empty claim and the probe found no off-frontier work. Compare with `tower_stalled`: same empty claim, opposite meaning.
'''
      navigateTo index
    }

    dispatcher -> waiting.towerStalled "WAITING … reason=tower_stalled waiting=2" {
      title "work exists and something is holding it — usually an open gate"
      notes '''
Gates are a custom type hidden from default queries, so N counts **work tickets**; a gate two levels below is invisible
in that number and is almost always the cause. Only a human closes a gate, so the loop writes this line once a minute
and waits — it does not end, retry, or nag.
'''
      navigateTo index
    }

    // ═ PART F — where all of it lands

    dispatcher -> logRun.fErrors "ERROR … (never rate-limited)" {
      title "every alarm, whoever saw it, in one file"
      notes '''
| File | Written by | Cadence |
|---|---|---|
| `claims` | the loop | every take and every put-back |
| `errors` | loop and both hooks | **every** failure, never capped |
| `waiting` | the loop | one a minute per reason |
| `closes` | the Stop hook | one per ticket landed |
| `failures` | the StopFailure hook | one per dead session |
| `results` | the loop | the tally, at `BO_MAX_TICKS` or Ctrl-C |
| `bd-stderr` / `claude-stderr` | the loop | the last tool stderr, quoted into the alarm |
| `spawn-<ticket>` / `done-<ticket>` | the loop / the worker | that worker's session id, and its own declaration |

**Reading a tower that is not moving**, in this order:

```bash
tail -f ~/.binary-obelisk/logs/<run>/waiting       # which reason, and how often
bd list --status in_progress --json                 # who is claimed, and by whom
bd show <id> --json                                 # its blockers — an open gate two levels below?
grep <ticket> ~/.binary-obelisk/logs/<run>/errors    # what refused, and at which step
claude agents                                       # is a session even alive?
```

Every line of every file carries the same nine keys, so any of these joins to any other by `grep` alone — and a
**missing** file is itself a finding: no `failures` line beside a `failed-agent` ticket is `status-not-set`.
'''
    }

    human -> beads "and every one of these is a person's call, not the loop's" {
      title "nothing here ever unclaimed, un-parked, or closed a gate"
      notes '''
The alarms are the design, not accidents: fifteen refusals and a fail-closed marker are the price of letting fourteen
unattended sessions with `bypassPermissions` commit onto shared branches. What they cost is a tower that stops and says
why, and waits for you.
'''
      navigateTo index
    }

    autoLayout LeftRight
  }
}
